<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-1-1-Propositions" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Propositions</title>

   <p>
    Mathematics and logic deal with statements that are either true or false.
  </p>

  <definition>
    <statement>
      <p>
        A <alert>mathematical statement</alert> or <alert>proposition</alert> is
        a statement that can be proven to be true or false.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <p>
    We start with some examples.
  </p>

  <example xml:id="exa-logic1">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Consider the following statements:
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>1+2=3</m>.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              An integer is divisible by two if and only if its last digit is
              <m>0, 2, 4, 6</m> or <m>8</m>.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              <m>8^{2}=60</m>.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              <m>2^{8}&lt;210</m>.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              <m>1&gt;2</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
        These are mathematical statements as each of them is either true or
        false. The first and the second statements above are true and the last
        two are false.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </example>

  <example>
    <statement>
      <p>
        We give now some examples which are not propositions.
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              Lebron James is a better basketball player than Michael Jordan.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Messi is better than CR7.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Taylor Swift is the greatest singer of all times.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
        All the sentences above are opinions and cannot be proven using logic to
        be true or false.
      </p>

      <p>
        Consider the following statements.
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              Lebron James has scored more points in his NBA regular career than
              Michael Jordan.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              As of 2026, Michael Jordan has won more NBA titles than Lebron
              James.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              As of 2026, CR7 has scored more goals than Messi.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Messi has won more World Cups than CR7.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>

      <p>
        The first statement above is true as Lebron James has scored
        <m>43440</m> points in his NBA regular seasons career while Michael
        Jordan has scored <m>32292</m> points.
      </p>

      <p>
        The second statement is also true as Michael Jordan has won six NBA
        titles while Lebron James has won four such titles.
      </p>

      <p>
        For the third statement, at the time of writing this book, CR7 has
        scored <m>976</m> goals while Messi has scored <m>921</m> goals. Hence,
        the third statement is also true.
      </p>

      <p>
        The fourth statement is also true because Messi has won one World Cup in
        2022 while CR7 has won none.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </example>

  <p>
    Statements are usually represented by letters, for example, <m>p, q, r</m>,
    etc and the truth value true is represented by <m>T</m> and false is
    represented by <m>F</m>. Similar to common language where we can form
    complex sentences, in the logical statement, we can also form a compound
    statement by logical operators.
  </p>
  <!--\begin{defn}\textbf{Compound statement} is a statement formed from two other statements in one of several ways. \end{defn}-->
  <p>
    We use <alert>truth tables</alert> to describe the relationships between the
    truth values of propositions. The simplest truth tables are below.
  </p>

  <blockquote>
    <tabular top="minor" left="minor">
      <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell><m>p</m></cell>
      </row>

      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell>T</cell>
      </row>

      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell>F</cell>
      </row>
    </tabular>
  </blockquote>

  <p>
    and
  </p>

  <blockquote>
    <tabular top="minor" left="minor">
      <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
      <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell><m>p</m></cell>
        <cell><m>q</m></cell>
      </row>

      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell>T</cell>
        <cell>T</cell>
      </row>

      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell>T</cell>
        <cell>F</cell>
      </row>

      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell>F</cell>
        <cell>T</cell>
      </row>

      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell>F</cell>
        <cell>F</cell>
      </row>
    </tabular>
  </blockquote>

  <definition xml:id="def-negation">
    <statement>
      <p>
        The negation of a proposition <m>p</m>, denoted by <m>\neg p</m> or not
        <m>p</m>, is true when <m>p</m> is false, and is false when <m>p</m> is
        true.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <example>
    <statement>
      <p>
        The negation of the proposition ``4 is an even number'' is ``4 is not an
        even number'' or ''it is not the case that 4 is an even number''.
      </p>

      <p>
        The negation of the proposition ``this car is blue'' is be the
        proposition ``this car is not blue''.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </example>

  <p>
    The following truth table summarizes the truth values for the negation.
  </p>

  <blockquote>
    <tabular top="minor" left="minor">
      <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
      <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell><m>p</m></cell>
        <cell><m>\neg p</m></cell>
      </row>

      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell>T</cell>
        <cell>F</cell>
      </row>

      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell>F</cell>
        <cell>T</cell>
      </row>
    </tabular>
  </blockquote>

  <definition>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>p</m> and <m>q</m> be two propositions. The proposition <m>p</m>
        and <m>q</m>, also denoted by <m>p\wedge q</m>, is true precisely when
        both <m>p</m> and <m>q</m> are true.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <example>
    <statement>
      <p>
        The proposition ``Delaware is the second smallest state in the United
        States and Dover is its capital'' is true as each of the statements
        ``Delaware is the second smallest state in the United States'' and
        ``Dover is its capital'' is true.
      </p>

      <p>
        The proposition `` <m>1+3 =4</m> and <m>5&gt;8</m> '' is false because
        the proposition `` <m>5&gt;8</m> '' is false .
      </p>
    </statement>
  </example>

  <p>
    The truth table below summarizes the truth values for <m>p\wedge q</m> with
    all possible truth values of <m>p</m> and <m>q</m>.
  </p>

  <blockquote>
    <tabular top="minor" left="minor">
      <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
      <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
      <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell><m>p</m></cell>
        <cell><m>q</m></cell>
        <cell><m>p\wedge q</m></cell>
      </row>

      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell>T</cell>
        <cell>T</cell>
        <cell>T</cell>
      </row>

      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell>T</cell>
        <cell>F</cell>
        <cell>F</cell>
      </row>

      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell>F</cell>
        <cell>T</cell>
        <cell>F</cell>
      </row>

      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell>F</cell>
        <cell>F</cell>
        <cell>F</cell>
      </row>
    </tabular>
  </blockquote>

  <p>
    In conclusion, the statement <m>p \wedge q</m> is true if both <m>p</m> and
    <m>q</m> are true and false if at least one of the statements <m>p</m> or
    <m>q</m> is false.
  </p>

  <example>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>p</m> be the statement
        <TODO type="unknown-macro"><!--todo: unknown macro "\em"--><c>\em</c></TODO>
        Alice has 2 erasers and <m>q</m> be the statement
        <TODO type="unknown-macro"><!--todo: unknown macro "\em"--><c>\em</c></TODO>
        Alice has 4 pencils. The compound statement <m>p\wedge q</m> is
        <TODO type="unknown-macro"><!--todo: unknown macro "\em"--><c>\em</c></TODO>
        Alice has 2 erasers and 4 pencils.
        <!--If the reality is Alice has 2 erasers and 4 pencils, then we say the statement $p\wedge q$ is true. Otherwise, if Alice has 2 erasers but doesn't have 4 pencils or vice versa, we will say that the statement $p\wedge q$ is false. Similarly, if Alice has neither 2 erasers nor 4 pencils, the conjunction $p\wedge q$ is also false. -->
      </p>
    </statement>
  </example>

  <p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          <alert>Disjunction (OR)</alert> <m>\vee</m> When two statements are
          related by a disjunction, we denote it by <m>p\vee q</m> and read as
          `` <m>p</m> or <m>q</m> ''. In this context, the ''or'' statement can
          sound a bit different from the ''or'' that we use in the daily life.
          If you are asked to choose a slice of cake or a scoop of ice cream,
          usually it means you can only pick one of them, but not both. However,
          in disjunction, choosing both is still considered true.
        </p>

        <example>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Let <m>p</m> be the statement ``Alice gets 2 erasers' and <m>q</m>
              be the statement ``Alice gets 4 pencils'', the compound statement
              formed by disjunction will be ``Alice gets 2 erasers or 4
              pencils.'' If the reality is Alice gets either 2 erasers or 4
              pencils, then the compound statement is true. The statement is
              only false if Alice doesn't get 2 erasers and 4 pencils.
            </p>
          </statement>
        </example>

        <p>
          The following truth table summarizes the truth values for the
          disjunction with all possible truth values of <m>p</m> and <m>q</m>.
        </p>

        <blockquote>
          <tabular top="minor" left="minor">
            <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
            <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
            <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
            <row bottom="minor">
              <cell><m>p</m></cell>
              <cell><m>q</m></cell>
              <cell><m>p\vee q</m></cell>
            </row>

            <row bottom="minor">
              <cell>T</cell>
              <cell>T</cell>
              <cell>T</cell>
            </row>

            <row bottom="minor">
              <cell>T</cell>
              <cell>F</cell>
              <cell>T</cell>
            </row>

            <row bottom="minor">
              <cell>F</cell>
              <cell>T</cell>
              <cell>T</cell>
            </row>

            <row bottom="minor">
              <cell>F</cell>
              <cell>F</cell>
              <cell>F</cell>
            </row>
          </tabular>
        </blockquote>

        <p>
          In conclusion, a disjunction statement <m>p\vee q</m> is true if
          <m>p</m> is true or <m>q</m> is true or both <m>p</m> and <m>q</m> are
          true. It is false when both <m>p</m> and <m>q</m> are false.
          Furthermore, the type of ''or'' that we used in daily life is called
          an ''exclusive or'' in logic, where the statement is true only when
          one of them is true but not both.
        </p>

        <example>
          <statement>
            <p>
              The compound statement `` <m>2^{2}=6</m> or <m>5&lt;6</m> '' is
              true while the statement `` <m>8</m> is an odd number or <m>8</m>
              is divisible by 3'' is a false statement.
            </p>
          </statement>
        </example>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          <alert>Implication/conditional proposition</alert>
          <m>p\rightarrow q</m> Can be read as:
          <alert><m>p</m> implies <m>q</m></alert>,
          <alert>if <m>p</m> then <m>q</m></alert>, <m>q</m> is necessary for
          <m>p</m>, and <m>p</m> is sufficient for <m>q</m>. We can also say:
          <m>p</m> is the hypothesis and <m>q</m> is the conclusion.
        </p>

        <p>
          Let <m>p</m>: ''The husband gets bonus'' and <m>q</m>: ''He buys a
          gift for his wife''. Then the implication <m>p\rightarrow q</m> is
          read as ''if the husband gets bonus, then he buys a gift for his
          wife''. Imagine if a person promises to get his wife a gift when he
          gets bonus, this promise is only broken if he gets bonus but not
          buying any gift. Otherwise, it is OK.
        </p>

        <p>
          The following truth table summarizes the truth values for the
          implication with all possible truth values of <m>p</m> and <m>q</m>.
        </p>

        <blockquote>
          <tabular top="minor" left="minor">
            <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
            <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
            <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
            <row bottom="minor">
              <cell><m>p</m></cell>
              <cell><m>q</m></cell>
              <cell><m>p\rightarrow q</m></cell>
            </row>

            <row bottom="minor">
              <cell>T</cell>
              <cell>T</cell>
              <cell>T</cell>
            </row>

            <row bottom="minor">
              <cell>T</cell>
              <cell>F</cell>
              <cell>F</cell>
            </row>

            <row bottom="minor">
              <cell>F</cell>
              <cell>T</cell>
              <cell>T</cell>
            </row>

            <row bottom="minor">
              <cell>F</cell>
              <cell>F</cell>
              <cell>T</cell>
            </row>
          </tabular>
        </blockquote>

        <p>
          <alert>Converse</alert> Given an implication <m>p\rightarrow q</m>, we
          can consider its converse which is <m>q\rightarrow p</m>.
        </p>

        <p>
          The following table compares the truth values of an implication and
          its converse.
        </p>

        <blockquote>
          <tabular top="minor" left="minor">
            <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
            <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
            <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
            <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
            <row bottom="minor">
              <cell><m>p</m></cell>
              <cell><m>q</m></cell>
              <cell><m>p\rightarrow q</m></cell>
              <cell><m>q \rightarrow p</m></cell>
            </row>

            <row bottom="minor">
              <cell>T</cell>
              <cell>T</cell>
              <cell>T</cell>
              <cell>T</cell>
            </row>

            <row bottom="minor">
              <cell>T</cell>
              <cell>F</cell>
              <cell>F</cell>
              <cell>T</cell>
            </row>

            <row bottom="minor">
              <cell>F</cell>
              <cell>T</cell>
              <cell>T</cell>
              <cell>F</cell>
            </row>

            <row bottom="minor">
              <cell>F</cell>
              <cell>F</cell>
              <cell>T</cell>
              <cell>T</cell>
            </row>
          </tabular>
        </blockquote>

        <p>
          An implication and its converse do not share the exact same truth
          value. This is a very important thing to note that whenever an
          implication is true, it does not directly imply that its converse is
          true. In the context of Mathematics, sometimes when a result is proven
          as an implication, mathematician will determine whether its converse
          is also true.
        </p>

        <example>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Let <m>x</m> be a real number. Consider the statement: If
              <m>x&gt;0</m> then <m>x^{2}&gt;0</m>. This is a true statement
              since the square of a positive real number is a real number.
              However, its converse, which is if <m>x^{2}&gt;0</m> then
              <m>x&gt;0</m> is not true. Take <m>x=-3</m> for example, then the
              value of <m>x^{2}=9 &gt;0</m> but <m>x&lt;0</m>.
            </p>
          </statement>
        </example>

        <p>
          In the situation where an implication and its converse are true,
          usually the result is presented as a double implication, which will be
          elaborated later in this section.
        </p>

        <p>
          Besides the converse, we also consider the contrapositive of an
          implication.
        </p>

        <p>
          <alert>Contrapositive</alert> If the implication is given by
          <m>p\rightarrow q</m> then its contrapositive is given by the
          implication <m>(\neg q) \rightarrow (\neg p)</m>.
        </p>

        <p>
          The following table compares the truth values of an implication and
          its contrapositive.
        </p>

        <blockquote>
          <tabular top="minor" left="minor">
            <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
            <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
            <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
            <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
            <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
            <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
            <row bottom="minor">
              <cell><m>p</m></cell>
              <cell><m>q</m></cell>
              <cell><m>\neg p</m></cell>
              <cell><m>\neg q</m></cell>
              <cell><m>p\rightarrow q</m></cell>
              <cell><m>\neg q \rightarrow \neg p</m></cell>
            </row>

            <row bottom="minor">
              <cell>T</cell>
              <cell>T</cell>
              <cell>F</cell>
              <cell>F</cell>
              <cell>T</cell>
              <cell>T</cell>
            </row>

            <row bottom="minor">
              <cell>T</cell>
              <cell>F</cell>
              <cell>F</cell>
              <cell>T</cell>
              <cell>F</cell>
              <cell>F</cell>
            </row>

            <row bottom="minor">
              <cell>F</cell>
              <cell>T</cell>
              <cell>T</cell>
              <cell>F</cell>
              <cell>T</cell>
              <cell>T</cell>
            </row>

            <row bottom="minor">
              <cell>F</cell>
              <cell>F</cell>
              <cell>T</cell>
              <cell>T</cell>
              <cell>T</cell>
              <cell>T</cell>
            </row>
          </tabular>
        </blockquote>

        <p>
          Since these two compound statements share the same truth value, we can
          say that they are equivalent. This fact will be useful in the next
          chapter when we are writing proof. Example: If I get a C- then I pass
          this course. This is equivalent as saying, ``if I do not pass this
          course, then I do not get a C-''.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          <alert>Double implication</alert> <m>p \Leftrightarrow q</m> (
          <m>p</m> if and only if <m>q</m> ) It means <m>p\rightarrow q</m> AND
          <m>q\rightarrow p</m>
        </p>

        <blockquote>
          <tabular top="minor" left="minor">
            <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
            <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
            <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
            <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
            <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
            <row>
              <cell/>
              <cell/>
              <cell/>
              <cell/>
              <cell><m>p\Leftrightarrow q</m></cell>
            </row>

            <row bottom="minor">
              <cell><m>p</m></cell>
              <cell><m>q</m></cell>
              <cell><m>p\rightarrow q</m></cell>
              <cell><m>q \rightarrow p</m></cell>
              <cell><m>(p\rightarrow q) \wedge (q\rightarrow p)</m></cell>
            </row>

            <row bottom="minor">
              <cell>T</cell>
              <cell>T</cell>
              <cell>T</cell>
              <cell>T</cell>
              <cell>T</cell>
            </row>

            <row bottom="minor">
              <cell>T</cell>
              <cell>F</cell>
              <cell>F</cell>
              <cell>T</cell>
              <cell>F</cell>
            </row>

            <row bottom="minor">
              <cell>F</cell>
              <cell>T</cell>
              <cell>T</cell>
              <cell>F</cell>
              <cell>F</cell>
            </row>

            <row bottom="minor">
              <cell>F</cell>
              <cell>F</cell>
              <cell>T</cell>
              <cell>T</cell>
              <cell>T</cell>
            </row>
          </tabular>
        </blockquote>

        <p>
          <alert>Conclusion:</alert> The double implication
          <m>p\Leftrightarrow q</m> is true if both <m>p</m> and <m>q</m> have
          the same truth value and it is false if <m>p</m> and <m>q</m> have
          different truth values.
        </p>

        <p>
          <alert>Example:</alert>
          <ol>
            <li>
              <p>
                ``An integer <m>x</m> is an even number if and only if <m>x</m>
                is evenly divisible by 2,'' is a true statement.
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                ``A two digits number is even if and only if it ends with
                <m>0</m>,'' is a false statement.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </p>

</section>